# The Next Chapter
#
# A score for a painted film about a life being lived: dawn on the sea, an old tree with a family
# under it, a kite on a windy hill, a coast road running on toward a lighthouse, a harbour lighting
# up at dusk, a path in the morning. D major at 88, six scenes of five bars landing on the downbeat,
# then a held D add9.
#
# It never stops moving: an ostinato runs from the first beat to the last, eighths on the electric
# piano at dawn and under the tree, resonant sixteenths through the pluck on the hill and the road,
# eighths again in the harbour, both together in the morning. The melody is sung by the Bocuma
# voice: one motif, a syncopated rise, stated under the tree, sequenced higher on the hill, opened
# into long gliding leaps on the road, turned low and questioning in the harbour, and brought home
# at the top in the morning. The bass walks between the roots. The drums change their minds with
# each scene: a whispered sixteenth pulse at dawn, a swung rim groove under the tree, syncopated
# kicks with ghosted snares and claps on the hill, a driving four-on-the-floor with lifted open
# hats on the road, a half-time in the harbour, everything in the morning; each scene rolls into
# the next.

bpm 88
gate 0.92
tail 6

section DAWN     0..20
section TREE     20..40
section KITE     40..60
section ROAD     60..80
section HARBOUR  80..100
section MORNING  100..120
section YOURS    120..132

